1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:09,798
IETF 127 Hackathon: CoAP over Bundle Protocol version 7, the Single Bundle demonstration.

2
00:00:11,248 --> 00:00:17,668
This demo carries CoAP application messages over BPv7, across intermittent links.

3
00:00:17,718 --> 00:00:21,402
The scenario is a Mars rover mission with three nodes.

4
00:00:21,452 --> 00:00:23,808
The Rover runs the CoAP client.

5
00:00:23,858 --> 00:00:27,073
The Satellite acts as a BPv7 relay.

6
00:00:27,123 --> 00:00:29,292
And Earth hosts the CoAP server.

7
00:00:30,692 --> 00:00:36,230
There is no proxy: CoAP processing happens only at the two application endpoints.

8
00:00:36,280 --> 00:00:40,478
First, the Rover encodes one CoAP Non-confirmable GET.

9
00:00:40,528 --> 00:00:45,075
Second, BPv7 transports that bundle through the Satellite.

10
00:00:45,125 --> 00:00:48,887
Third, Earth replies in a separate BPv7 bundle.

11
00:00:48,937 --> 00:00:54,738
The Satellite simply forwards bundles. No application-layer CoAP proxy runs there.

12
00:00:56,138 --> 00:01:01,327
This is Mars Rover Mission Control, the graphical interface we built for the demo.

13
00:01:01,377 --> 00:01:03,955
First, select the Single radio button.

14
00:01:04,005 --> 00:01:09,730
In this baseline, NSTART is 1, so only one interaction is outstanding at a time.

15
00:01:11,130 --> 00:01:15,537
Next, press Send GET for the Rover Safety application, just once.

16
00:01:15,587 --> 00:01:21,513
The Rover submits a Single: one CoAP Non-confirmable request, inside one bundle.

17
00:01:22,913 --> 00:01:28,188
The application log shows the request leaving the Rover, and the response from Earth.

18
00:01:28,238 --> 00:01:34,883
Earth replies with 2.05 Content, and the matching Token correlates the response with the request.

19
00:01:34,933 --> 00:01:41,221
In this GUI test, the application round-trip time is 50.22 milliseconds.

20
00:01:41,271 --> 00:01:46,520
Note that this GUI run is separate from the Wireshark capture we look at next.

21
00:01:47,920 --> 00:01:51,282
Now, the real Wireshark capture from the Satellite.

22
00:01:51,332 --> 00:01:56,338
With the bpv7 display filter, we see four bundle observations.

23
00:01:56,388 --> 00:02:01,985
Frames 11 and 22 are the request: in from the Rover, and out towards Earth.

24
00:02:02,035 --> 00:02:07,337
Frames 39 and 58 are the response, travelling back on the return path.

25
00:02:08,737 --> 00:02:12,878
Our Lua dissector decodes the Single request in frame 11.

26
00:02:12,928 --> 00:02:20,395
It shows exactly one CoAP member: a Non-confirmable GET, with a 24-bit message ID and a Token.

27
00:02:20,445 --> 00:02:23,880
The Uri-Path is mission / safety-guidance.

28
00:02:23,930 --> 00:02:28,124
And, as required for a Single, there is no Payload-length option.

29
00:02:29,524 --> 00:02:33,490
Frames 11 and 22 carry the same bundle identity:

30
00:02:33,540 --> 00:02:39,488
the same source, ipn:1.10, the same creation time, and sequence number 1.

31
00:02:39,538 --> 00:02:42,556
So the Satellite forwards the same request bundle.

32
00:02:42,606 --> 00:02:45,238
The bundle payload is 54 bytes.

33
00:02:45,288 --> 00:02:53,495
And Wireshark confirms a BPv7 lifetime of 100,000 milliseconds: 100 seconds.

34
00:02:54,895 --> 00:02:57,532
Frame 39 is Earth's response.

35
00:02:57,582 --> 00:03:02,924
It is a separate CoAP Non-confirmable message, with code 2.05 Content.

36
00:03:02,974 --> 00:03:06,968
It carries the same Token as the request in frame 11.

37
00:03:07,018 --> 00:03:11,196
That matching Token identifies the request–response pair.

38
00:03:12,596 --> 00:03:14,761
Let's summarise what we verified.

39
00:03:14,811 --> 00:03:20,415
Single mode sends one CoAP Non-confirmable GET, with no Payload-length option.

40
00:03:20,465 --> 00:03:24,398
NSTART is 1, in both the GUI and the Rover node.

41
00:03:24,448 --> 00:03:28,603
Earth answers with 2.05 Content, and the Token matches.

42
00:03:28,653 --> 00:03:35,167
Wireshark shows the same BPv7 bundle on both hops, with a 100-second lifetime.

43
00:03:35,217 --> 00:03:41,446
The Single functional demonstration is complete. Aggregate testing is a separate experiment.

44
00:03:42,896 --> 00:03:49,413
Thank you for watching. The code and the Wireshark dissector are in our project repository.
